WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.320
Memory. It can be both a blessing and a curse.&nbsp;
You’ll always remember that beautiful moment when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.240
you and your family walked across golden sands by&nbsp;
the sea on your favorite childhood vacation. Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:18.000
first kiss. The time you discovered your favorite&nbsp;
movie, TV show, or book. But you’ll also always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.600
remember the time you said “You too”, when the&nbsp;
movie theater usher told you to enjoy the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.040
or when you first got your heart broken, or the&nbsp;
first time you lost a friend or family member,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.800
and realized someday that&nbsp;
others would be mourning you.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.480
Or that time a giant, unkillable&nbsp;
lizard tried to eat you alive.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:43.040
This is why the SCP Foundation has Amnestics -&nbsp;
The course of miracle drugs that erase memories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.840
Whether they’re synthetic or taken from&nbsp;
a giant, godlike eel miles under the sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.880
Amnestics are the way the Foundation erases&nbsp;
things that are better not remembered.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.880
But what would you like to forget?

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.640
We put that question out to the&nbsp;
wonderful fans of SCP Explained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.360
and you delivered, telling us about all the&nbsp;
anomalies or anomalous situations that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.320
so horrifying you’d want the Foundation to&nbsp;
neuralise the living hell out of you after an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:15.200
encounter. So let’s jump right in and see what’s&nbsp;
better off stricken from our mental records…

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:22.000
Random User said, “For me, it would be Scp-1733,&nbsp;
why you may ask? Well, the overall thought of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.000
being trapped inside of a repeating cycle&nbsp;
where you eventually break into insanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.160
is scary to think about. I’d get my memory wiped&nbsp;
any day of the week.” And we’re off to a strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:37.760
existentially-horrifying start. Unless you’re a&nbsp;
superfan of the Boston Celtics and the Miami Heat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.320
being trapped in an endlessly recursive&nbsp;
tape of the 2010 NBA season opener would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:48.160
be a memory you’d want to be erased. But man,&nbsp;
that dunk from Robinson? An excellent play.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.080
BioticNinja said, “There’s a lot&nbsp;
I would voluntarily use amnestics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.480
for even without encountering SCPs; what&nbsp;
makes you think I would have the mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.040
willpower to resist taking a few if I so&nbsp;
much as encountered such a horrid thing?”

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.880
We appreciate your honesty. And to be fair, how do&nbsp;
we know that you haven’t used Amnestics already?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:13.040
You could have used it so many times to delete&nbsp;
so many unpleasant experiences. How many gaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.800
black holes, exist in your recollections? Have&nbsp;
you ever gone somewhere new and gotten that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.080
nagging sense you’ve been there before? Perhaps&nbsp;
you have. Just be sure to like this video and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.280
leave a comment saying “Don’t forget, I was here&nbsp;
before”, in case you delete this memory too.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.680
William Clarke said, “Honestly I wish I&nbsp;
could take amnestics after just watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.960
this channel's well-executed&nbsp;
depiction of the red reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.880
and then reading comments that elaborated&nbsp;
on some of the more graphic details.”

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.840
Well, that’s incredibly kind of you, and we’re&nbsp;
equally disturbed by the nightmarish nature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.840
SCP-3001, the place where Dr. Scranton&nbsp;
has been trapped for uncountable years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.120
losing his body and mind. Sadly for Scranton&nbsp;
himself, there’s no way we can ever get in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.560
there to give him some Amnestics himself,&nbsp;
but we’re sure he appreciates the sentiment.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:08.400
False Alias said, “Definitely the Bone hive and&nbsp;
the flesh that hates. I already hate bugs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.840
disease. Last thing I need for my mental health&nbsp;
is to know those are running around somewhere.”

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.400
While Amnestics will solve a secondary encounter&nbsp;
with the Bone Hive earwigs or the Flesh That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.680
Hates, we’re sad to tell you that if you are the&nbsp;
victim of either of these anomalies, no amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.200
amnestics will soften the swirling void of horror&nbsp;
your life will soon become. Though it might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:34.080
funny to see a horrific Flesh Monster stumbling&nbsp;
around and wondering where it left its car keys.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:40.960
True Void Chaos said, “SCP-1762, that was&nbsp;
one of the few SCPs that brought me to tears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.160
and despite having never met them&nbsp;
I really miss those dragons.”

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.600
Thanks for reminding us about&nbsp;
that one, True Void Chaos.&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.160
Now we’re feeling sad again too…&nbsp;
Oh god, where are the Amnestics!?

00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:00.400
Sarah Jamesina Sarah said, “I feel like I’d use&nbsp;
those if I ever participated with the “desire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.520
camera.” I don’t think I have any dark secret&nbsp;
desires or that I’m evil or anything like that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.200
(although the possibility of being shown&nbsp;
my inner shadow has crossed my mind,)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.360
I worry that seeing my “true desire”&nbsp;
might make me too narrow-minded.”

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.960
I’m sorry to tell you this, Sarah, but&nbsp;
we’ve already played out this scenario.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:20.000
We took a photo of you with the&nbsp;
Desire Camera, and you were so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.960
utterly disturbed by the horrifying nature of&nbsp;
your true desire, you immediately requested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:31.120
Amnestics. But we still know what you want, and&nbsp;
we’re considering drafting a restraining order…

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.240
Frost King said, “The Yule Man is one of the SCPs&nbsp;
that have given me chills to even think about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.920
If it was real, I might have to&nbsp;
choose ignorance on this one boys.”

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.000
Now this is a dangerous one. After&nbsp;
all, when the winter months draw in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.880
and you’re settling down to enjoy the Christmas&nbsp;
Season, you might notice strange noises in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.240
attic. Funny smells coming from outside your&nbsp;
house. Darting shadows on the yard. The feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.840
that you’re being watched. Maybe you’ll just try&nbsp;
to push those feelings down and enjoy the holiday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:04.080
because you don’t know about the&nbsp;
danger lurking - Until it’s too late.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.400
Ross Albatross said, “I'd want to&nbsp;
forget about the bowl of soup from dad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.120
The emotional triggers I personally have, as&nbsp;
someone who lost their father as a kid, run deep.”

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.400
We’re incredibly sorry to hear&nbsp;
that, Ross, and we hope you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:25.040
able to find peace - or something close to&nbsp;
it - someday. Losing someone is never easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.880
and we hope you have friends and family&nbsp;
in your life there to support you.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:35.040
Jay Lego Ninjago said, “The Maybe There&nbsp;
Monsters. Their idea even gives me chills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:41.600
Monsters that can harm you yet you are the only&nbsp;
one able to see them. Also, scp 2508, a house you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.560
can't leave until you die that has happened&nbsp;
to many foundation workers including 05-7.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:51.760
The idea of being trapped forever in a place&nbsp;
without any human contact just creeps me out.”

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:57.680
Both are indeed extremely disturbing concepts,&nbsp;
and could be things you’re eager to forget. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.720
sometimes it’s best to keep those frightening&nbsp;
memories for your own safety. To quote Rafiki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:08.800
the past can hurt, but you can either run from it
or learn from it.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.960
SuitoSpy said, “While I feel like many&nbsp;
people will go with better known SCPs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:20.880
I would have to go with SCP-1981. Seeing that&nbsp;
tape would be a scary thing to have to remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.800
and personally, I would want to&nbsp;
rid of it as quickly as possible.”

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:28.480
Interestingly, the aides of Ronald&nbsp;
Reagan himself also thought it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:32.800
best to give him amnestics after seeing the&nbsp;
Ronald Reagan Cut Up While Talking tape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.160
but because they didn’t give him the proper&nbsp;
amount, it wound up destroying his mind. So yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.920
we suppose the moral of the story&nbsp;
is to use amnestics responsibly.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:47.920
Juan Pereyra said, “As Much as i love 682 as a&nbsp;
fictional monster, the mere idea of a life-hating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.640
violent hyper-adaptable and unkillable&nbsp;
atrocity would just be too much to bear.”

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.080
If it makes you feel any better,&nbsp;
given how much he hates everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.880
SCP-682 would probably take an&nbsp;
amnestic to forget you, as well.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:04.320
Max Powers - the name you love&nbsp;
to touch - said, “I think just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.720
watching what happens to the D class on a&nbsp;
daily basis would make me want to forget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:12.000
So many of those deaths would&nbsp;
be very traumatic to watch.”

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.240
That is incredibly true. Despite&nbsp;
the fact that many of the D-Classes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:21.200
are themselves violent criminals, the way the&nbsp;
Foundation treats them is undeniably inhumane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:26.320
and horrific. While it’s easy to make a “greater&nbsp;
good” argument about all of their terrible fates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:32.160
as an empathetic human being, it can’t be easy&nbsp;
to watch all this play out again and again.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.200
KingMe131 said, “I'd probably go with&nbsp;
something like the Sarkicist cultists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.600
If I were to encounter them I'd probably be&nbsp;
close to death cause I love our evolution and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:46.400
growth with tech and those guys would probably do&nbsp;
all sorts of twisted things to me before the SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:51.600
foundation can stop it or anything involving the&nbsp;
children of the scarlet king for similar reasons.”

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:56.320
An interesting thought: Sometimes, encountering&nbsp;
groups of interest can be even more frightening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:00.960
than encountering the anomalies themselves.&nbsp;
Sarkicists will twist your body into the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.360
nightmatish configurations imaginable, and&nbsp;
the Children of the Scarlet King might use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:09.040
dark rituals to impregnate you with&nbsp;
more of their dark deity’s progeny.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:13.760
So… Yeah, both of these call&nbsp;
for some sweet, sweet amnestics.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.720
Jean G said, “I feel like I wouldn't&nbsp;
really ever submit myself to amnestics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.040
I have a really morbid curiosity that makes&nbsp;
it so the more awful of a thing I see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:27.200
the more I want to understand it. So yeah,&nbsp;
an MTF member is probably gonna have to stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.520
me or else I'd become a paranoid lunatic&nbsp;
trying to figure out how Anomalies work.”

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.120
Once again, we appreciate the&nbsp;
honesty and self-awareness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:40.080
It’s very possible the Foundation would somehow&nbsp;
trick you into undergoing amnestic treatments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.320
so your investigations don’t become a thorn&nbsp;
in their side. And failing that, they might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:49.760
just do things the old fashioned way and send&nbsp;
an MTF hit-squad to silence you permanently.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.960
Yeet Yeeter said, “I feel as if&nbsp;
having an encounter with SCP-087,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:58.480
since the only three documented cases&nbsp;
have left the class d prisoners in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:01.360
state of shock or never to be heard of again.”

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:06.000
Another very wise choice.&nbsp;
SCP-087, and more specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.880
the entity hiding within&nbsp;
this bottomless staircase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:14.240
leaves even its surviving victims in a state of&nbsp;
permanent catatonia. Hopefully, amnestic treatment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:19.440
would allow you to regain your faculties and live&nbsp;
something resembling a normal life afterwards.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:22.320
Richard Womack said, “096,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:27.360
I'm curious if the application of amnestics in&nbsp;
a timely manner would quell its murderous rage.”

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:32.320
The SCP Foundation has been hoping to find this&nbsp;
out for some time now, actually. If you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:39.360
SCP-096 coming after you, then you may as well&nbsp;
take an amnestic. If it works, awesome! If not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:44.240
at least you’ll spend a few minutes in a state of&nbsp;
sweet blissful ignorance before being torn apart.

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:48.160
Joaquin Palacios said, “For me the Panacea SCP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:52.080
mostly so that way I don’t live with the&nbsp;
guilt of knowing that there is a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:56.720
prevent death from illness or injury,&nbsp;
even if there is no non-anomalous cure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.840
In short, if I knew about the Panacea, I would&nbsp;
go mad trying to figure out a way to obtain it.”

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:07.040
Now this is an interesting one. So many&nbsp;
people are sadly suffering from diseases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:11.040
with no non-anomalous cure, and if&nbsp;
the Panacea was ever made public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.560
it would surely become a kind of El Dorado for&nbsp;
many suffering or wishing to profiteer from it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:21.360
Wars would be started. People would turn&nbsp;
on each other. The hunt would be endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:27.120
and all-consuming. Perhaps it would be better&nbsp;
to forget than to taunt us with false hope.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:31.920
Rahma Shifa said, “SCP-4975. I'm definitely gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:36.320
need amnestics after hearing about that&nbsp;
absolute terrible excuse for an SCP.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:42.480
It's just so disturbing. If you don't know what&nbsp;
SCP-4975 is, then save yourself the trouble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:48.800
Don't go searching about 4975. You'll&nbsp;
regret it. Don't say I didn't warn you.”

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:51.360
Well, personally, we don’t think&nbsp;
there’s that much to worry--&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:53.200
Wait, does anyone else hear that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:57.920
Huh, I don’t remember if I brought a clock in&nbsp;
here. Must be a side effect of the amnestics…

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:03.440
Dischord Nation said, “Scp 023: The&nbsp;
Black Shuck is one that comes to mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:06.640
Maybe the amnestics can&nbsp;
cancel the anomalous effects.”

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:12.000
There’s only so far you can try to outrun fate.&nbsp;
If you look into the eyes of the Black Shuck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.160
either you or a family member of&nbsp;
yours is doomed to die. You can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:21.200
yourself forget if it would bring you some&nbsp;
comfort, but it will happen all the same…

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:22.480
Hon Guy said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:29.040
“I’d say SCP-106, if by some miracle I survived&nbsp;
his pocket dimension relatively unharmed.”

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:35.440
If you did, by some miracle, manage to survive&nbsp;
being trapped in SCP-106’s horrific pocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.920
dimension, it’s unsurprising that&nbsp;
you’d need some amnestic therapy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:43.360
Though it would leave you wondering why&nbsp;
you’ve got all those scars, and missing limbs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:49.760
and missing eyes, and missing teeth. The Old&nbsp;
Man certainly leaves his mark on his… friends.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:56.640
Norbies said, “I’d probably take amnestics from&nbsp;
seeing SCP 2030 (which I think is laugh is fun)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:00.560
It just has so many disturbing episodes&nbsp;
that would make me want to forget it.”

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:05.360
What!? That’s ridiculous! Why would you ever want&nbsp;
to forget any classic episodes of Laugh Is Fun?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:10.160
That show is hilarious. Especially&nbsp;
the Margaret Thatcher episode!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.560
Some people just have no sense of humor…

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:16.640
Darkmega5 said, “That one fish that&nbsp;
can hunt you from any body of water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:19.840
if you know about it. Can’t get&nbsp;
hunted if I forget about it.”

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:23.280
SCP-1128, also known as the Aquatic Horror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:28.400
is the one thing more likely to give you&nbsp;
hydrophobia than contracting rabies. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:33.280
amnestics is believed to be one of the few ways&nbsp;
you might be able to escape its jaws of death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:38.080
Still, you probably won’t want to spend&nbsp;
some time at the local pool any time soon.

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:42.880
GamingwolfZJ said, “If I survived the&nbsp;
encounter, I’d definitely give myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:49.920
amnestics if I encountered SCP 3166. The idea of&nbsp;
getting attacked by a guy dressed up as an overly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:55.120
obese ginger cat while grabbing rotten lasagna&nbsp;
from its gullet just to shove it into my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.120 --> 00:13:00.240
is an image I would definitely use SCP&nbsp;
Foundation Certified Brain Bleach for.”

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:05.760
Look, Gaming Wolf, I get that almost being force&nbsp;
fed human flesh lasagna until you die by a mutant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.160
commercialized homonculus of a popular comic strip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:11.440
character is a traumatic experience&nbsp;
that will leave you shaken up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:17.280
but is that any excuse to fat-shame&nbsp;
Garfield? Come on now, let’s stay civil here…

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:21.840
8-Ball Gaming said, “If I saw someone&nbsp;
being forced through SCP-957’s chest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:25.360
cavity then I would literally&nbsp;
become a Class-D to unsee it.”

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:29.120
Suit yourself, buddy. I actually think&nbsp;
it’s an extremely entertaining sight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.760
To each their own, I suppose.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:35.440
Loot pad67 said, “Being in 3008.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:39.920
It's such a terrifying thought that I would&nbsp;
much prefer leaving it to my fantasy.”

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:43.520
Being trapped in the Infinite Ikea&nbsp;
is a truly terrifying thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:47.840
and one you might want to forget after&nbsp;
escaping. That being said, being trapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:53.200
in a regular Ikea is also pretty terrifying. You&nbsp;
can really lose a whole saturday trying to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:58.640
a new desk in that garish, Swedish, flat-pack&nbsp;
hellscape. Can I get some of those amnestics?

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.560
Daniel Jestice said, “The living Legos, so&nbsp;
that way every time I see them it will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.840
as if it is my first time seeing&nbsp;
them and always make me happy.”

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:11.120
Well, that’s certainly a delightful&nbsp;
notion. We appreciate you giving us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:15.280
this wholesome suggestion in amongst the&nbsp;
rest of the horrifying trauma, Daniel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:20.640
it’s very much appreciated. We’ll give you a&nbsp;
few doses of amnestics as a “thank you” gift.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:25.760
Izzy Stardust said, “Not so much an SCP, but&nbsp;
a certain procedure we don't talk about.”

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:30.800
Thankfully, it is actually standard procedure to&nbsp;
be given amnestics after performing Procedure-110&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:36.160
Montauk to preserve its high level of&nbsp;
secrecy, but it does have its downsides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:39.360
For example, you forget what chapter&nbsp;
you were up to in the storybook.

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:45.680
Nick T Wolf Discusses said, “I would take it&nbsp;
if I met Clef, that guy sounds like no fun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:49.440
With the odd way he is, I think&nbsp;
he might be an unknown SCP.”

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.000
Sadly, we don’t believe that Dr. Clef&nbsp;
is anomalous, he’s actually just a jerk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:57.920
We still wouldn't blame you for wanting to&nbsp;
take some amnestics after meeting him, though.

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:00.480
Grand Wyrm said, “Well to be honest I would use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:05.360
it after watching a good movie or show to&nbsp;
watch it again, the same goes for books.”

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:09.760
That’s a fascinating approach. For what it’s&nbsp;
worth, there are a lot of wonderful movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:14.560
shows, and books out there - and a lot of&nbsp;
wonderful YouTube videos here on SCP Explained.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:18.880
You might be limiting your scope by repeatedly&nbsp;
wiping your memory and consuming the same pool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:24.480
of media again and again in perpetuity. But then&nbsp;
again, maybe you’re already stuck in that loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:28.800
and any good media you discover and enjoy&nbsp;
in the future is just something you’ve seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:33.360
and enjoyed, potentially hundreds&nbsp;
or even thousands of times before.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:35.520
Aren’t amnestics a little creepy like that?

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:38.800
And there you have it, folks,&nbsp;
some of your weirdest, wildest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:42.640
and most traumatic suggestions for all&nbsp;
the anomalies worth forgetting - Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:46.960
remembering is just too painful. Thank&nbsp;
you for sending in your thoughts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:51.120
and if you think we missed any, please let us&nbsp;
know down in the comments - And keep an eye on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:55.920
our community tab for more chances to take&nbsp;
part in the next community response video!

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:58.880
In the meantime, we’ll… Uh… I’m sorry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:03.920
what are we doing here again? I’m getting so&nbsp;
forgetful these days, might be my medication…

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:09.120
Now go check out “SCP-049 - What Actually is&nbsp;
the Pestilence? The Plague Doctor Questions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:15.120
Theories” and “SCP-096 - Look at a Picture of Shy&nbsp;
Guy in Space? The Shy Guy Questions and Theories”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:18.960
for more questions and answers&nbsp;
from the SCP Explained community!

